<chapter xml:id="ch-split-apply-combine">
	<title>Split, Combine, Apply a DataFrame</title>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			A common situation with data analysis is to have a dataset and you want to compare means or standard deviations
			within a dataset.
			What needs to often happen is that you first split a dataset, do some analysis on each group then summarize.
			This is know as <em>split-apply-combine</em>.
			We will demonstrate this with a few examples.
			We will also use some larger datasets to show the power of this technique.
			Before getting started, there are two datasets:
		</p>

		<p>
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						Let's return to the <c>iris</c> dataset that we loaded in the previous chapter.
						Recall that the <c>RDatasets</c> package must be loaded and then load the dataset with
					</p>

					<program language="julia">
						<code>
          iris = RDatasets.dataset("datasets","iris")
						</code>
					</program>

					<p>
						The results are not shown here, but you should see the top and bottom of the dataset which is 150 lines long.
					</p>
				</li>

				<li>
					<p>
						A large database of <em>Simpsons</em> episodes.
						This was originally on
						<url href="https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/prashant111/the-simpsons-dataset/code">this Kaggle page</url> and was
						downloaded and saved as a CSV file in the <c>assets/datasets</c> directory.
					</p>

					<p>
						We will load the dataset directly off the web with the following code.
						Note: you will need to download the <c>CSV</c> package and add <c>using CSV</c>.
					</p>

					<program language="julia">
						<code>
          simpsons_episodes = CSV.read(Downloads.download("https://pstaabp.github.io/sci-comp-book/external/datasets/simpsons_episodes.csv"), DataFrame)
						</code>
					</program>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>

		<p>
			The rest of this chapter is split into two parts.
			The top is using the command built-in to <c>DataFrames</c> and the second part uses the techniques from
			<c>DataFramesMeta</c>.
			If you prefer the latter, then skip to the bottom of this chapter.
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<section xml:id="sect-split-apply-combine">
		<title>Split-Apply-Combine with DataFrames</title>

		<p>
			First, we will split the dataset by the <c>Species</c> column using the <c>groupby</c> function in the <c>@chain</c>
			block as
		</p>

		<program language="julia">
			<code>
				@chain iris begin
					groupby(:Species)
				end
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			and the result shows two DataFrames (actually <c>GroupedDataFrame</c> s), the first and last.
			At the top of the output, it says <c>GroupedDataFrame with 3 groups based on key: Species</c> Even though we have
			``split'' the dataset, it is all stored in a single variable of type <c>GroupedDataFrame</c>.
			It shows part of the first group and part of the last group.
			This is mainly because of space for the output.
		</p>

		<p>
			Next, we may want to compare the variables between these three groups.
			For example, to compare the mean of the <c>SepalLength</c> variable, we use the <c>combine</c> function like:
		</p>

		<program language="julia">
			<code>
				@chain iris begin
					groupby(:Species)
					combine(:SepalLength =&gt; mean)
				end
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			and the result is
		</p>

		<p>
			<cd>
				<cline>3×2 DataFrame</cline>
				<cline>Row  Species      SepalLength_mean</cline>
				<cline>     Cat…         Float64</cline>
				<cline>1    setosa       5.006</cline>
				<cline>2    versicolor   5.936</cline>
				<cline>3    virginica    6.588</cline>
			</cd>
		</p>

		<p>
			If we want the number of rows in each set:
		</p>

		<program language="julia">
			<code>
				@chain iris begin
				  groupby(:Species)
				  combine(nrow)
				end
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			which shows that there are 50 rows in each subset.
			Notice that this is different than the previous example (mean of the SepalLength) in that no column is needed and the
			function <c>nrow</c> is applied to the entire Grouped Data Frame.
		</p>

		<p>
			We can also use <c>combine</c> to produce many summarizing values.
			If we want the number of rows, the mean of the SepalLength, the standard deviation of the SepalWidth and the maximum
			of the PetalWidth, we can do this with one command as in
		</p>

		<p>
			<cd>
				<cline>@chain iris begin</cline>
				<cline>  groupby(:Species)</cline>
				<cline>  combine(nrow, :SepalLength =&gt; mean, :SepalWidth =&gt; std, :PetalWidth =&gt; maximum)</cline>
				<cline>end</cline>
			</cd>
		</p>

		<p>
			resulting in
		</p>

		<p>
			<cd>
				<cline>3×5 DataFrame,</cline>
				<cline>Row   Species     nrow     SepalLength_mean  SepalWidth_std  PetalWidth_maximum</cline>
				<cline>      Cat…        Int64    Float64           Float64         Float64</cline>
				<cline>1     setosa      50       5.006             0.379064        0.6</cline>
				<cline>2     versicolor  50       5.936             0.313798        1.8</cline>
				<cline>3     virginica   50       6.588             0.322497        2.5 </cline>
			</cd>
		</p>
	</section>

	<section xml:id="sec-dfm-split-apply-combine">
		<title>Split-Apply-Combine with <c>DataFramesMeta</c></title>

		<p>
			This section goes over the same examples as above using the macros from <c>DataFramesMeta</c>.
			Before proceeding, make sure the following is loaded:
		</p>

		<program language="julia">
			<code>
          using DataFramesMeta, Statistics
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			We start with trying with grouping the <c>iris</c> dataset by the <c>Species</c> column.
			This is done in <c>DataFramesMeta</c> with the <c>@groupby</c> macro.
			Within a <c>@chain</c> block, this looks like:
		</p>

		<program language="julia">
			<code>
				@chain iris begin
					@groupby(:Species)
				end
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			which will make 3 grouped data frames.
			The output will show only part of the first and last one.
			In this case, each grouped data frame has 50 rows.
		</p>

		<p>
			If we want to find the mean sepal length of each grouping, we use the <c>@combine</c> macro and define what operation
			we want on each group.
			Specifically this can be done with:
		</p>

		<program language="julia">
			<code>
				@chain iris begin
					@groupby(:Species)
					@combine(:mean_SepalLength = mean(:SepalLength))
					end
				end
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			The result of this will be
		</p>

		<p>
			<cd>
				<cline>3×2 DataFrame</cline>
				<cline>Row  Species      mean_SepalLength</cline>
				<cline>     Cat…         Float64</cline>
				<cline>1    setosa       5.006</cline>
				<cline>2    versicolor   5.936</cline>
				<cline>3    virginica    6.588</cline>
			</cd>
		</p>

		<p>
			In the <c>@combine</c>, the general form is <c> :col = f(:col1, :col2, ...)</c>, where the left side of the
			expression will be the name of the column in the summarized DataFrame and then the function is applied to each
			specified column.
		</p>

		<note>
			<p>
				Recall that using <c>@combine</c> in <xref ref="sec-dfm-summarize"/> resulted in a DataFrame with a single row.
				Since we have used <c>@combine</c> on a grouped DataFrame, we get a row for each grouping.
				In this case 3.
			</p>

			<p>
				Additionally, Note that we did not use the broadcasting/dot notation with the function in this because we want to
				apply the function to an entire column (or vector).
				This is a key difference, to using functions in other commands like <c>@select</c> or <c>@transform</c>.
			</p>
		</note>

		<p>
			Next, we perform a number of summaries at the same time.
			For each species, we'd like to know:
			<ol>
				<li>
					<p>
						number of observations/data points
					</p>
				</li>

				<li>
					<p>
						Mean of the Sepal Length
					</p>
				</li>

				<li>
					<p>
						Standard Deviation of the Sepal Length
					</p>
				</li>

				<li>
					<p>
						Maximum Petal Width
					</p>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>

		<p>
			We can do this with the following:
		</p>

		<program language="julia">
			<code>
				@chain iris begin
					@groupby(:Species)
					@combine(:num = length(:SepalLength), :mean_SepalLength = mean(:SepalLength), :std_SepalLength = std(:SepalLength), :max_PetalWidth = maximum(:PetalWidth))
				end
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			and make sure that you get all of the line with <c>@combine</c> on it.
			It's quite long.
			The result is:
		</p>

		<p>
			<cd>
				<cline>3×5 DataFrame</cline>
				<cline>Row   Species     num     mean_SepalLength   std_SepalLength   max_PetalWidth</cline>
				<cline>      Cat…        Int64   Float64            Float64           Float64</cline>
				<cline>1     setosa      50      5.006              0.35249           0.6</cline>
				<cline>2     versicolor  50      5.936              0.516171          1.8</cline>
				<cline>3     virginica   50      6.588              0.63588           2.5</cline>
			</cd>
		</p>

		<p>
			Because the <c>@combine</c> lines can get long as you add information to the summary, another form for this is using
			a <c>begin/end</c> block.
			The above command can be written out differently as
		</p>

		<program language="julia">
			<code>
				@chain iris begin
					@groupby(:Species)
					@combine begin
						:num = length(:SepalLength)
						:mean_SepalLength = mean(:SepalLength)
						:std_SepalLength = std(:SepalLength)
						:max_PetalWidth = maximum(:PetalWidth)
					end
				end
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			which is clearly easier to read.
			<c>DataFramesMeta</c> also has a shortcut for the situation above, in which the dataframe is split and then
			summarized.
			This is the <c>@by</c> macro.
			The above can be written as:
		</p>

		<program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
			<code>
				@chain iris begin
					@by :Species begin
						:num = length(:SepalLength)
						:mean_SepalLength = mean(:SepalLength)
						:std_SepalLength = std(:SepalLength)
						:max_PetalWidth = maximum(:PetalWidth)
					end
				end
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			where the <c>@by</c> command with both split (as in <c>@groupby</c> ) and summarize.
			The column after the <c>@by</c> will group the dataframe by that variable.
			Then the <c>begin/end</c> block is the same as the <c>@combine</c>.
		</p>

		<p>
			As another example, we will turn to the <c>simpsons_episode</c> dataset that you should have loaded.
			To begin, let's take the dataset, split by <c>original_air_year</c> and then within each of theses, find the number
			of episodes and the average <c>imdb_rating</c>.
			We will use the <c>@by</c> command to do this compactly:
		</p>

		<program language="julia" line-numbers="yes">
			<code>
        @chain simpsons_episodes begin
          @by :original_air_year begin
            :num_per_year = length(:number_in_season)
            :rating = mean(:imdb_rating)
          end
        end
			</code>
		</program>

		<p>
			The top of this is:
		</p>

		<p>
			<cd>
				<cline>28×3 DataFrame</cline>
				<cline>Row  original_air_year   num_per_year   rating</cline>
				<cline>     Int64               Int64          Float64?</cline>
				<cline>1    1989                1              8.2</cline>
				<cline>2    1990                21             7.89524</cline>
				<cline>3    1991                25             8.12</cline>
				<cline>4    1992                23             8.21739</cline>
				<cline>5    1993                21             8.32857</cline>
				<cline>6    1994                23             8.18261</cline>
				<cline>7    1995                25             8.424</cline>
				<cline>8    1996                22             8.25455</cline>
				<cline>9    1997                27             8.10741</cline>
			</cd>
		</p>

		<p>
			Let's examine this carefully.
			The dataset in interest is the <c>simpsons_episodes</c> and then we will form groups on the <c>original_air_date</c>.
			The block that goes with the <c>@by</c> is the summary section.
			Each line will result in a different summary.
			The <c>num_per_year</c> will calculate how many are in each group (or year).
			The <c>rating</c> is calculated as the mean of the <c>imdb_rating</c>.
		</p>

		<p>
			Note that this dataset is only through 2016, so is missing since then.
		</p>
	</section>
</chapter>